@prefix zz:   <http://example.org/orexis/zamioculcas#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/zamioculcas#> .
@prefix water: <http://example.org/orexis/water#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .
@prefix unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .

<http://example.org/orexis/zamioculcas> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Zamioculcas zamiifolia" ;
    rdfs:comment """One species, its own package, layered on the water domain. The shape a plant
should take, and it is deliberately the same shape a PART takes: a species is a model and the pots
are the units, exactly as a DHT11 is a model and the soldered ones are units. So what the species
knows is stated ONCE on the class and reaches every pot by entailment, and a world that acquires a
second ZZ writes one triple. See knowledge/decisions/a-part-is-described-once-and-fitted-many-times.md.

Depends on `water:` — unlike a part package, which must not. A thermistor resolves to 1 degree in
any world that will ever use one, so binding it to a domain would be backwards; a plant species IS
the domain, and the properties it cares about are the domain's to name.""" .

:ZamioculcasZamiifolia a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf water:Plant ;
    rdfs:label "Zamioculcas zamiifolia" ;
    schema:alternateName "ZZ plant" , "Zanzibar gem" , "emerald palm" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:88189-1> ;
    rdfs:comment """An aroid from the dry grassland and forest floor of eastern Africa — Zanzibar
south to South Africa — and everything below follows from where it is from. It stores water in
RHIZOMES, thick underground stems, which is why it survives being forgotten and why it is killed by
attention: the failure mode is not drought but rot, and the plant looks well right up until the
rhizome has gone.

Slow-growing and famously tolerant of low light. Sap contains calcium oxalate crystals and is a
skin and mucous-membrane irritant, so it is a poor choice within reach of a cat or a toddler —
recorded here because it is a fact about the species someone caring for one should meet, even
though nothing in this repository can act on it.""" .

#################  What it needs, and what it will merely survive  #################
#
# TWO ranges, and SSN has both words. `ssn-system:OperatingRange` is where the plant does well;
# `ssn-system:hasSurvivalRange` is where it does not die. For most things that distinction is
# decoration. For this one it is the entire care instruction, because the gap is asymmetric: the
# dry end of survival is enormous and the wet end is a cliff.
#
# The codebase already gestured at exactly this split without a term for it — `water:bandLow` is
# documented as "a comfort limit, desire-relative — not the rot limit, which is physical". The rot
# limit is the survival range's ceiling, and it is a property of the SPECIES rather than of anyone's
# desire, so it belongs here.
#
# Stated on the class as `owl:hasValue`, so the ranges are ONE pair of individuals every ZZ shares
# — which is what a species fact is. A per-pot fact (how fast THIS pot dries) is not here; that is
# `water:driesPerDay`, stated per plant in a world, because it is about the pot and not the
# species.

:ZamioculcasZamiifolia rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn-system:hasOperatingRange ;
      owl:hasValue :IndoorOperatingRange ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn-system:hasSurvivalRange ;
      owl:hasValue :IndoorSurvivalRange ] .

:IndoorOperatingRange a ssn-system:OperatingRange ;
    rdfs:label "Zamioculcas indoors, growing season" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where it grows rather than merely persists. Named for the regime it assumes, because a second one is the obvious extension and would need telling apart from this: in winter this plant genuinely wants less water, not the same water less often. What stops that being written today is recorded in knowledge/decisions/the-range-is-the-plants-and-the-pick-is-the-agents.md — ssn-system:inCondition cannot say which of its conditions is the qualifier and which the requirement, and nothing here could select a regime anyway." ;
    ssn-system:inCondition
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:SoilMoisture ;
          schema:minValue 0.10 ; schema:maxValue 0.30 ; schema:unitCode unit:UNITLESS ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:AirTemperature ;
          schema:minValue 18.0 ; schema:maxValue 26.0 ; schema:unitCode unit:DEG_C ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:AirHumidity ;
          schema:minValue 0.40 ; schema:maxValue 0.60 ; schema:unitCode unit:UNITLESS ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:Illuminance ;
          schema:minValue 1000.0 ; schema:maxValue 10000.0 ; schema:unitCode unit:LUX ] .

:IndoorSurvivalRange a ssn-system:SurvivalRange ;
    rdfs:label "Zamioculcas indoors, what it will tolerate" ;
    rdfs:comment "Wider at both ends and NOT symmetric, which is the fact worth having in a machine-readable form. It will come back from bone dry; it does not come back from a rotted rhizome, so the ceiling is the number that matters and the floor is nearly free. The temperature floor is where leaf damage starts rather than where it dies, and it is not frost hardy at any point." ;
    ssn-system:inCondition
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:SoilMoisture ;
          schema:minValue 0.02 ; schema:maxValue 0.45 ; schema:unitCode unit:UNITLESS ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:AirTemperature ;
          schema:minValue 12.0 ; schema:maxValue 35.0 ; schema:unitCode unit:DEG_C ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:AirHumidity ;
          schema:minValue 0.20 ; schema:maxValue 0.90 ; schema:unitCode unit:UNITLESS ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Condition , schema:PropertyValue ; ssn:forProperty water:Illuminance ;
          schema:minValue 100.0 ; schema:maxValue 20000.0 ; schema:unitCode unit:LUX ] .

# Every figure is written with a decimal point, including the whole ones. `18` is an xsd:integer
# in Turtle and `18.0` is an xsd:decimal, and the plant shapes ask for decimal — so a temperature
# written the natural way fails validation for a reason that has nothing to do with temperature.
# Caught by tests/test_species.py and not by any world, because no world had planted one yet.

#################  Where these figures come from, and where they do not  #################
#
# The TEMPERATURE and HUMIDITY figures are ordinary published care guidance for this species and
# are in the units the guidance itself uses.
#
# The SOIL MOISTURE figures are not, and the difference should be visible rather than buried. No
# horticultural source states a volumetric fraction for a houseplant — care guidance says "let it
# dry out almost completely", and this is that sentence put on the 0-1 scale of saturation that
# `water:SoilMoisture` is denominated in. The ORDERING is the durable claim and is what a reader
# should trust: a ZZ wants to sit drier than a succulent does, far drier than a fern, and its
# ceiling is a cliff rather than a preference. The exact numbers are a calibration and belong to
# whoever measures a real pot with a real probe.
#
# LIGHT is now stated rather than described, and it needed a term first. `water:Illuminance` went
# into the DOMAIN and not into this package: light is nobody's species in particular, and
# `zz:Illuminance` would have made every other plant borrow it from the Zamioculcas.
#
# Its provenance differs from the other three and is the part most likely to be over-trusted.
# The LUX BANDS are a convention rather than a measurement — "low light", "bright indirect" and
# "direct sun" are the categories every houseplant source is written in, and the conventional
# ranges for them are roughly 250-1000, 2500-10000 and 10000+ lux. What is guidance about THIS
# plant is where it sits in those bands: it does well from medium to bright indirect, tolerates
# low light better than almost any houseplant sold, and scorches in direct sun. The numbers here
# are that placement read off the convention, so the bands are the claim and the endpoints are a
# reading of them.
#
# Note what is now true and was not: nothing in this repository measures light. That is fine and
# is why it was worth stating anyway — a range for an unmeasured property is a checkable fact
# waiting for an instrument, where a sentence in a comment is not. It becomes live the day a
# light sensor appears, with no edit here.
#
# STILL ABSENT, and for a reason no term would fix: the sap is a calcium-oxalate irritant. There
# is nothing in this system that fact could inform, so it stays in the class comment where a
# person will read it.
